en  and we'll answer as many questions as we  have time for so let's go ahead and get  started our first question  you mentioned 21 cfr part 11 data  integrity could you please explain again  how many error can lead to data  integrity challenges  for sure definitely so yeah human error  if it creeps in in the test in the  configuration of the instrumentation um  it can cause problems with data  integrity now in that fda's alcoa  acronym as i mentioned in the  presentation the final a in the alpine  acronym refers to the accuracy of the  electronic record itself so we're not  talking about security or or  anything like that it's really about the  data in the electronic record  so human error and toc analyze  configuration  and lead to errors in the analyzer  results  so the electronic record itself although  secure  is not accurate it doesn't represent an  accurate analysis so i gave an example  how hugh and eric can creep into toc  measurements just now  when i explained how users of some  typical toc analysts have to guess the  amount of toc in the water sample in  order to dose the correct amount of  sulfate  so  the temptation is not to put too much  sulfate in because that runs the risk of  creating bubbles and under reporting the  toc in the sample but that potentially  leads to accuracy issues in the  electronic record unfortunately the qbd  measurement is not affected by this  problem  thank you tony our next question  you mentioned that with some toc  analyzers the technician has to guess  how much of oxidizer  the personal fate to add to the sample  based on a guess of the toc content in  the sample  but how can they guess the toc content  before they make a measurement  that that's exactly the point really  it's kind of a chicken and egg thing if  you excuse the analogy because you have  to know  how much toc is in the sample  to add the sulfate to then measure the  toc so it's a challenge  and it faces many technicians using toc  analyzers today but unlike some commonly  used qc lab toc analyzers that measure  tiers to measure organic carbon sorry  dissolved in the water sample using  permeable membranes  as part of the measurement process which  is what caused which is what causes the  issues with sulfate the qbd 1200  measures carbon dioxide from the organic  carbon in gas phase it doesn't use  permeable membranes so the presence of  gas bubbles does not interfere with the  measurement  hence when we're measuring whether we're  measuring  purified water or water rejection we can  safely add up the sulfate every time  to analyze up to 4000 ppp of toc without  interfering with the measurements in any  way at all  and of course as toc is a limit test  with the maximum limit being 500 parts  per billion the ability to oxidize  oxidized to 4000 ppb ensures that any  excursions above the 500 ppb limit would  be captured and reported  thank you tony we have one more question  here for you  thank you for your presentation it is  well known that some laboratory toc  analyzers measure both inorganic carbon  and total carbon using separate sensors  and then use these two measurements to  calculate  toc frequently resulting in errors in  the reported toc value  even sometimes reporting negative toc  results due to sensor drift could you  please explain how the qbd 1200 avoids  these types of errors  uh thank you that's a good question  and i think that the basic is that  unlike some  laboratory toc analyzers the qbd 1200  removes the inorganic carbon from the  sample first and measures that  directly  and then oxidizes the toc and measures  the carbon dioxide from that  so it's directly making a measurement of  toc using a single sensor which has an  accuracy of plus or minus two percent so  because it's using the single centering  tooth instead of two different sensors  it's not possible for the qed 1200 to  report a negative to  toc result because it measures toc  directly rather than calculating it from  two separate measu